X:1
T:Dyma Wyliau Hyfryd Llawen
T:Here is a Splendid Joyful Festival
R:carol
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=5 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by Robert Thomas Evans (born 1880), a farmer, of Perthyfelin, Dinas Mawddwy, Meirionnydd; recorded 25.10.60 (St Fagans tape 306). He learnt the carol at home, and it was known locally as "Hen Garol Perthyfelin" because his family sang it so often at the plygain services in the area. The tune was called "Y Ceiliog Du" (The Black Cockerel). Only six verses were recorded; the rest come from a family notebook. The author of the words is unknown; they are probably nineteenth-century, and dwell more on the Crucifixion and Resurrection than on the Nativity.
N:The quaver that ends bars 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11, 14 and 15 is bracketed on the score, as it is only sung when a verse has a syllable for it. Small notes on the score split the crotchet F in bars 5 and 12 into two quavers, and the last F of bar 2 into two semiquavers, for verses with extra syllables. The slurs in bars 11 and 15 are dotted on the score.
